No. The pool is leaking connections, and a larger pool would only take longer to fill up.

The leak is in `withClient` in `db.js`:

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, `client.release()` never runs, so that client stays checked out for good. Each failed query permanently takes one of the 20 slots. `pool.log` fits this: `idle=0` the whole time, and `waiting` keeps climbing (7, 11, 14, 19, 23, 28) until requests start hitting `timeout exceeded when trying to connect`. Under ordinary heavy load, the waiting count would rise and fall as clients were returned.

The release has to happen in a `finally` block so it runs on errors too. Also, `pool.log` only covers about 2.5 minutes (14:02:11 to 14:04:42), not the full hour. If the full log shows `idle` going back up at some point, a few of those slots were being returned after all, but the code bug is still there either way.

No. This looks like a connection leak, and a bigger pool would only take longer to fill up.

In `db.js`, `withClient` only calls `client.release()` when `fn` succeeds. If `fn` throws, that client is never returned to the pool. Each failed query permanently removes one of the 20 connections.

`pool.log` fits that pattern. The pool stays at `total=20 idle=0` while `waiting` climbs steadily from 7 to 28 and never drains. Real load would show some idle connections between bursts. The log only covers 14:02 to 14:04, not a full hour, but the trend is clear.

Release the client in a `finally` block so it goes back to the pool even when `fn` throws:

```js
async function withClient(fn) {
  const client = await pool.connect();
  try {
    return await fn(client);
  } finally {
    client.release();
  }
}
```

Also check whether any code calls `pool.connect()` directly without going through `withClient`, because those calls could leak connections the same way.
